# Publishing a service from this VM

Managed by CINC. This file is rewritten on every converge; edits are lost.

**You do not need to publish anything, and by default you should not.** Anything
you publish is reachable from the entire internet, and its hostname becomes
public knowledge within minutes — every certificate issued is written to public
Certificate Transparency logs, which are monitored continuously. A temporary
test host is not obscure.

## Your hostname

    https://<%= @fqdn %>

One hostname per VM. Several services can share it by path.

## Publishing

Attach your container to the `web` network and give it these labels:

```yaml
services:
  myapp:
    image: your/image
    restart: unless-stopped
    networks: [web]
    labels:
      - traefik.enable=true
      - traefik.http.routers.myapp.rule=Host(`<%= @fqdn %>`)
      - traefik.http.routers.myapp.entrypoints=websecure
      - traefik.http.routers.myapp.tls.certresolver=le
      - traefik.http.services.myapp.loadbalancer.server.port=8080
      - traefik.http.routers.myapp.middlewares=auth@file

networks:
  web:
    external: true
```

Set `loadbalancer.server.port` to the port your app listens on *inside* the
container. There is no reload step — Traefik picks the container up by itself.

**`restart: unless-stopped` is not optional here.** This VM is stopped when you
stop working and started again when you come back, and Docker only brings back
containers that asked to be brought back. Without it your service is up until
the end of the day and down every morning, with nothing to tell you why. On
`docker run`, the same thing is `--restart unless-stopped`.

## Password protection

`auth@file` is the last label above and it is **strongly recommended**. A
password has already been generated for you:

```bash
cat ~/.config/traefik/password        # username: dev
```

To add more users:

```bash
htpasswd -nbB -C 12 alice 'their-password' >> ~/.config/traefik/users
systemctl --user restart traefik
```

The restart is required: Traefik reads the password file when its middleware
configuration loads, and appending a line to `users` does not touch that
configuration, so nothing changes until it reloads.

Without the `auth@file` label your service is open to anyone who finds the
hostname, and what you expose is your responsibility.

## When something does not work

Almost every failure looks the same from outside — the browser reports an
invalid certificate. The real reason is always here:

```bash
journalctl --user -u traefik -n 50
```
